Ladybird tells each WebContent or WebWorker process the permission
states of only the sites it shows. A process asks the UI process the
first time it reads a site’s state, and gets an answer only for a
top-level origin it hosts a document of (or, for a worker, the worker’s
own top-level site) — and from then on, only those sites’ changes.

Gecko does the same: ContentParent::AboutToLoadDocumentForChild() calls
TransmitPermissionsForPrincipal(), which sends a content process the
permissions for the keys of the principals it loads, and the content
process’s PermissionManager::PermissionAvailableInternal() refuses a
key it hasn’t received. Blink and WebKit keep no permission state in
the renderer at all — every query is a message to the browser or UI
process, which checks the origin against the sender: In WebKit, the
UI process’s WebPermissionControllerProxy::query() checks the claimed
ClientOrigin with ProcessCommittedClientOrigin, while in Blink, the
PermissionServiceImpl::GetPermissionResult() takes it from the frame’s
RenderFrameHost. Ladybird keeps a per-site cache in the renderer
instead, so that a read stays synchronous, as the spec’s permission
state algorithm requires.
